          '<p>A website brief is a few pages of answers a company prepares before it asks an agency or a freelancer for a price. It explains what the business does, what the new site is for, who will use it and what it has to include. It is not a technical specification. It is the starting point for the first conversation and for the proposal.</p>',
          '<p>Without a brief, every vendor guesses. One agency prices five pages with no copywriting, another prices twenty pages with copy and a photo shoot. Both proposals say “company website,” yet you can’t compare them. A brief levels the playing field: every vendor works from the same facts, so price differences reflect their approach, not their assumptions.</p>',
          '<p>A good brief also cuts the cost of surprises. The expensive items are the ones discovered halfway through: an integration nobody mentioned, a second language “for later,” copy that doesn’t exist yet, or an old site with Google traffic whose URLs disappear at launch. Each of them can be priced up front if it is in the brief, so the schedule and budget cover the full scope from day one.</p>',
